potion (0.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Removed the external libabz and libdebug dependencies. libdebug's
    logging is now a small stderr/syslog shim in src/log.[ch] and its
    memory leak tracker is gone, potion using malloc(3) directly. The
    two libabz macros potion needed are in src/util.h.
  * libabz's intrusive list was deliberately not carried over: despite
    its BSD notice it is verbatim Linux 2.4 include/linux/list.h, which
    is GPL-2.0, together with Simon Tatham's MIT list sort stripped of
    its copyright notice. Vendoring it would have made this package's
    licensing statement untrue. src/flow.c now uses the BSD
    <sys/queue.h> macros from libc and qsort(3) instead, so potion
    contains no third-party code at all.
  * Security: the IPv4 parser was handed the pre-decapsulation capture
    length, so every bounds check was short by the link-layer header and
    a crafted or truncated frame could be read up to 16 bytes past the
    captured data, with the stray bytes rendered as addresses and ports.
  * Security: an IPv4 header length below the fixed 20 bytes (including
    zero) passed the length check and was then used as an offset.
  * Security: headers are copied out of the capture buffer instead of
    being accessed through casts, which has no alignment guarantees.
  * Security: privileges are dropped once the capture handle is open,
    so the full-screen session no longer runs as root when potion was
    started through sudo or from a set-user-ID binary.
  * When the flow table filled up the newest flow was evicted instead of
    the oldest, so no new flow ever survived. Eviction is now least
    recently used.
  * A datagram whose first fragment arrived before its later fragments
    created a second, permanently incomplete flow. Fragment matching is
    now driven by a separate FLOW_PARTIAL flag.
  * The average bitrate was computed by dividing by the flow age, which
    is zero for any flow created in the current second.
  * Fixed an out-of-bounds read of the rate suffix table, a format
    string with more conversions than arguments in the syslog facility
    diagnostic, an unchecked localtime(3) result, and several width
    checks defeated by signed/unsigned conversion on narrow terminals.
  * pcap_dispatch() is given a packet budget so a busy interface can no
    longer starve the display, the timers and signal delivery, and a
    capture error now shuts potion down instead of leaving it spinning
    on a frozen screen.
  * The terminal is restored on SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGFPE, SIGILL and
    SIGABRT by replaying the terminfo teardown strings and the saved
    line discipline, using only async-signal-safe calls. Resizes are
    handled, and signal dispositions are saved and restored with
    sigaction(2) rather than forced to SIG_DFL.
  * A terminal that hangs up while potion is running is detected rather
    than spun on, and the key handler has a budget like the capture.
  * Refuse to start unless stdin and stdout are terminals, and report
    when the terminal is too small to lay out rather than drawing
    outside it.
  * 802.1Q and 802.1ad tagged frames are decoded rather than dropped.
  * --snaplen is range checked, -h/--help prints to stdout and exits
    successfully, --flows accepts its own default, and new --version and
    --no-promisc options were added.
  * Replaced the recursive make framework with a single Makefile that
    honours CC, CFLAGS, CPPFLAGS and LDFLAGS from the environment, finds
    its libraries with pkg-config, and no longer strips the binary
    itself or forces -Os over the packaging flags.
  * Modernised the packaging: debhelper compat 13 via build dependency,
    the dh sequencer, hardening build flags, a machine-readable
    copyright file, and a test run that is skipped when cross building.
  * Added a test suite covering the packet parser and the flow table,
    run at build time.
  * The flow a first fragment merged into kept the datagram id of
    whichever datagram created it, so every fragmented datagram after
    the first on a five-tuple stranded its own later fragments in a
    partial flow that nothing could complete.
  * Packets are no longer parsed past the end of the IP datagram, and
    the wire length is preferred over the length the sender advertises
    wherever the two can differ for reasons other than link padding.
  * The count of packets dropped or not understood is displayed rather
    than only counted.
  * Corrected the man page, which documented a log file option that does
    not exist and the wrong defaults for --flows and --inactive.
  * This release supersedes the unreleased 0.0.6.

 -- Abraham van der Merwe <abz@frogfoot.com>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 23:35:13 +0200

